# TypeScript Strict Style

Keep Playwright code strictly typed. Prefer compiler-checked types over assertions.

## Rules

- Do not use `any`. Use `unknown` and narrow it.
- Give public page-object methods and exported helpers explicit return types (`Promise<void>`).
- Prefer `interface` for object shapes; use `type` for unions, intersections, and mapped types.
- Name types `PascalCase`, values/functions `camelCase`, env-backed constants `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`.
- Avoid `as` except when narrowing is impossible; never use it to silence errors.
- Enable and respect `strict` in `tsconfig.json`.

## Example

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
async login(email: any, password: any) {
  await this.email.fill(email);
}

// ✅ GOOD
async login(email: string, password: string): Promise<void> {
  await this.email.fill(email);
  await this.password.fill(password);
  await this.submit.click();
}

function parseBody(value: unknown): { error?: string } {
  if (typeof value === 'object' && value !== null) {
    return value as { error?: string };
  }
  return {};
}
```
